== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
hash function ≠ encryption
ordinary hash ≠ cryptographic hash
public-key cryptography ≠ blockchain
digital signature ≠ human identity / intent
blockchain ≠ cryptocurrency
blockchain ≠ every distributed ledger
consensus ≠ majority vote / unanimity
smart contract ≠ legal contract by definition
blockchain token ≠ cryptocurrency by definition
wallet ≠ location where ledger assets are stored
decentralized identity ≠ blockchain identity
DID ≠ person
zero-knowledge proof ≠ encryption / application anonymity
DeFi ≠ absence of intermediaries or governance
AMM ≠ constant-product mechanism only
liquidity pool ≠ AMM
liquidity provision ≠ risk-free yield
constant product ≠ fixed price
concentrated liquidity ≠ Uniswap by identity
arbitrage ≠ risk-free execution in real markets
slippage ≠ price impact
slippage tolerance ≠ realized slippage
price impact ≠ fee
oracle ≠ truth source
attestation ≠ truth
</pre>

== Source families ==

* NIST FIPS 180-4 / 202 — hash functions.
* NIST public-key terminology and FIPS 186-5 — asymmetric cryptography and signatures.
* NIST IR 8202 — blockchain, consensus, smart contracts, wallets, oracles.
* NIST IR 8301 — token design, wallets, blockchain identity/zero-knowledge context.
* W3C DID Core and Verifiable Credentials — decentralized identifiers, claims, credentials and attestations.
* Goldwasser–Micali–Rackoff — zero-knowledge foundations.
* BIS DeFi/AMM reports and working papers — market structure, liquidity, execution and risks.
* Angeris/Chitra and Uniswap v2/v3 technical papers — constant-function and concentrated-liquidity mechanics.
* Kyle / Shleifer–Vishny — price impact and limits to real-world arbitrage.

